Given \(\sigma(\underline{\mathit{Q}}^{\prime}\), \(\overline{\mathit{Q}}^{\prime}\), \(\overline{\mathit{\chi}}\)) corresponding to a point in the Fundamental Rectangle, as well as \(g_{2}\) and \(g_{3}\) or the equivalent, find \(z\).

Only a few significant figures are obtainable from the use of any of the given (truncated) reversed series, except in a small neighborhood of the center of the series. For greater accuracy, use inverse interpolation procedures.

If the given function does not correspond to a value of \(z\) in the Fundamental Rectangle (see Conformal Maps) the problem can always be reduced to this case by the use of appropriate reduction formulas in **18.2.** This process is relatively simple for \(\underline{\mathit{Q}}(z)\), more difficult for the other functions (e.g. if \(\Delta\)>0 and \(\underline{\mathit{Q}}=a+ib\), where \(b\)>0, simply consider \(\overline{\mathit{Q}}\)=\(a-ib\) and find \(z_{1}\) in \(R_{1}\)**[Figure 18.1];** then compute \(z_{1}\)=\(\overline{z}_{1}\)+\(2\mathit{\omega}^{\prime}\), the point in \(R_{1}\) corresponding to the given \(\underline{\mathit{Q}}\)).

